<div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-16">
  <section class="grid items-center gap-10 md:grid-cols-[0.8fr_1.2fr] md:gap-14">
    <img src="/images/nick.jpg" alt="Nick Ostrovsky on a freediving boat" width="900" height="900" class="aspect-square w-full rounded-2xl object-cover">
    <div>
      <p class="mb-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-600 dark:text-sky-400">Nick Ostrovsky</p>
      <h1 class="mb-5 mt-0 text-4xl font-black tracking-tight sm:text-6xl">I train attention in the water.</h1>
      <div class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
        <p>I spent twenty years building software. I still do. Freediving did not make me an ex-developer. It gave me a second way to use the same attention: through the body, under real pressure, away from the screen.</p>
        <p>I came from extreme sports. In freediving I train the same attention in a calmer form: breath, equalization, movement, safety and recovery.</p>
        <p>I train and teach from Phuket, Thailand, in English and Russian.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto my-16 max-w-3xl">
    <p class="mb-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-sky-600 dark:text-sky-400">What I teach</p>
    <h2 class="mb-5 mt-0 text-3xl font-black sm:text-4xl">Notice the signal. Choose the action.</h2>
    <div class="prose text-lg">
      <p>Freediving gives direct feedback. The body gives a signal. The diver notices it and chooses the next action.</p>
      <p>That sequence is what I teach. Notice, relax, equalize, turn, recover.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

